there's a new piece called if you're  rich you're more lucky than smart and  there's math to prove it that has been  making the rounds the last day or so and  it's very well written although it's a  little bit short on sort of like this  actual science and data backing up that  claim but it's a really interesting  topic that I want to talk about because  it's something that I've been talking  about for a long time and that's that  we like to think of our society as a  meritocracy right one where people have  what they've earned and what they  deserve because of the actions they've  taken and the decisions they've made and  their talent and their intelligence and  so on and so forth and especially if  you're a defender of sort of the  unregulated so-called free market  capitalism you want to push this idea  right that the system if it's unequal  its unequal because people have what  they deserve people have what they have  earned and it's a way to sort of  rationalize or feel better about the  insane inequality that we have in our  society that being said most reasonable  people would understand that that's not  exactly right that it doesn't really  just work out that everybody gets  exactly what they deserve and it's based  on talent or intelligence or whatever  other metric you want to point to so for  an example when you look at class  mobility right we know that upward  mobility is really low in the United  States even equally smart people with  similar parenting situations and  adjusting for other factors if you're  born poor it is much more likely that  you will end up living a life of a poor  person or a lower middle class person  than if you were to be born rich we just  know that right and it disconnects from  the distribution that we have of  intelligence or so-called talent or  industriousness or whatever factor you  want to look at but once you accept that  there's still this idea that if you do  become rich whether you started poor or  lower middle class or middle class or  upper middle class that if you end up  rich you did it again on your merits  you've worked hard or you're smart or a  combination of the two and there's now a  study from the University of Catania  in Italy which looks at the role of  chance in life success or we might say  it looks at the role of luck some people  call it or randomness we can use these  terms sort of interchangeably for our  discussion here and what sort of got  professor Alessandro platino looking at  this is that he noticed as many other  people have that there's a sort of 8020  distribution of wealth that 20% of the  people have about 80% of the wealth but  that the skills and the talent seemed to  follow a much more standard distribution  bell curve pavatt pattern so then simple  mathematics questions why it is that  that would be the case if there is not  some element of randomness right what  accounts for the concentration of wealth  in a way that separates or doesn't track  distribution of talent and intelligence  because it is really really unequal like  I gave you the 8020 number twenty  percent of people have 80 percent of the  wealth the richest 1% have about 50  percent of the world wealth and what  this study and others have found is that  increasingly the answer is luck luck  doesn't literally need to mean luck like  casino luck type of thing which I  believe is just odds of course but it  includes that elements of what we might  call luck or odds such as the timing of  when you start a business or when you  graduate with a particular degree that  puts you on a path to prosperity of  sorts there's a lot of these factors  that you don't control if you had been  born two years earlier or two years  later the same talent the same  characteristics would not have led to  the same prosperity that they might have  in the real world luck can also just be  having born to a rich family right that  that's a huge predictor of wealth were  you born to a rich family that's a  different sort of type of Locker I guess  we'd call it chance and what Professor  platino did is to run some simulations  and rerun them time and again  distributing wealth to people on the  basis of certain levels of skill  intelligence ability talent and the  factor that ended up creating the  distributions that we see in the real  world right this extreme inequality is  to add an element of randomness  which we could attribute to like I said  timing right place right time rich  parents whatever else the case may be  every time that Professor platino and  his team reran the simulation the  results differed slightly but what was  always the case was that the wealthiest  20% were not the most talented 20% and  it was so lopsided that randomness which  in the real world we call locker timing  or whatever played a huge role and it  pushed some people into the top in terms  of wealth regardless of ability and it  kept a ton of people with no shortage of  ability or intelligence or  industriousness out of being top earners  so there's no question that this one  study isn't all-encompassing it doesn't  account for every variable right but  increasingly we are learning that we  simply do not have a meritocracy as many  people think that we do and this relates  to one of the underlying principles of  my political philosophy which is where I  end up disagreeing with lots of people  on the right if you believe that there  is a direct one-to-one relationship  between effort and success or between  intelligence and success you are willing  to accept certain economic systems only  when you realize that your success  although it might seem inevitable or  obvious to you was the result of in  great part chance or timing or odds or  luck whatever word you want to use you  should if you're a reasonable person  rethink how society should be organized  and this connects to one of the most  interesting political philosophies which  is a theory of justice by John Rawls  Rawls published a theory of justice in  1971 and the brilliant thing about a  theory of justice is that it suggests if  we were to come together to figure out  how to organize society right if we said  let's meet and say forget about how  society is organized let's come up all  together on a plan we should do it from  what Rawls calls an original position  which means if I come to the table to  figure out how to organize society I  don't come in and design society  to work for me if I'm already David  Pakman the owner of an established  business with a few employees and I  already own a house right I need to  design a society that I would accept if  I were back at zero and I didn't know  what my luck of the draw would be would  I be born into a very poor family or  very rich family would I be born  somewhere with more economic  opportunities or fewer and the premise  is that if you don't know which straw  you will draw in life you're going to be  much more incentivized to actually think  about what sort of an organization makes  sense regardless of whether you're rich  or upper middle class or middle class  but for everybody it's a thought  experiment for sure Pat and I think it's  an interesting one I don't know if  you're familiar with the Rawls's theory  of justice but increasingly luck plays a  big role in success yes I am familiar  with the theory of justice and for Rawls  the solution wasn't free market or  everyone being equal it was providing a  strong social welfare system and still  allowing that upward mobility and to the  broader point you know maybe luck should  be a factor because we want to encourage  entrepreneurship we want to encourage  risk-taking but it shouldn't be the top  thing I mean intelligence how hard you  work that should rank at the top I think  yeah and actually I would argue even if  you don't have those things you should  still actually have an opportunity to  live a good life and I think that's the  flip side of it that sometimes we see  this when we say success shouldn't  depend on luck it should depend on skill  but are we saying that if you happen to  be born lacking particular skills or  predispositions to do well in certain  areas that you shouldn't have a good  life I would say absolutely not and none  of this is to say people don't earn what  they have right it's about recognizing  that doing what it takes to earn things  can work or not work based on  circumstances that are beyond your  control and we should be aware of that  when we think about how to organize our  world and part of the resistance that  people have to thinking about society  that's with this way is they don't want  to admit or maybe they don't believe or  maybe they don't know that chance plays  a huge role in where you end up in life  nobody really wants to believe that  particularly if you've done well if more  people knew that they'd be much more  willing to consider how society should  organized again if they didn't know what  they're placed within it was going to be  checkout john rawls a theory of justice  really really important document and  really nothing surprising in this study  whatsoever the video you just finished  watching was made possible by you  through the membership program you can  sign up for membership at david  pakman.com slash membership we are  viewer supported independent media you  can use the coupon code  I voted 17 for a 40% discount off of  your membership
 

